WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
Transport security protects confidentiality and integrity between a gRPC client and service.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:15.880
Because gRPC normally runs over HTTP/2, production services should have deliberate connection-level security rather than relying on network placement or a development default.

3
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.260
The channel configuration should match the sensitivity of the data and the environment where the service runs.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:28.580
TLS should be the production baseline for network calls unless there is a clearly controlled and documented infrastructure reason for a different design.

5
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:40.360
HTTPS endpoints and certificate validation help the client establish that it is talking to the expected service and help protect messages while they cross networks, proxies, and platform layers.

6
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.640
Client certificate authentication can support mutual service identity in appropriate service-to-service environments.

7
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.940
In that model, the service can validate the client certificate as part of caller identity.

8
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:59.400
This can be useful in controlled enterprise, platform, or service mesh designs, but it requires operational ownership rather than one-time setup.

9
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.520
Certificate lifecycle matters.

10
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:13.060
Trust anchors, certificate issuance, environment separation, renewal, rotation, revocation, monitoring, and failure behavior all need owners.

11
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:20.680
A service that depends on certificates should have a clear plan for what happens before expiration, during rotation, and when validation fails.

12
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.000
Transport security also depends on infrastructure.

13
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:37.620
Reverse proxies, ingress controllers, load balancers, service meshes, and hosting platforms may terminate or re-establish TLS, enforce HTTP/2 settings, or forward identity-related information.

14
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:43.720
The gRPC team should know where transport security starts and ends in the deployed path.

15
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.520
The baseline is not just to enable TLS somewhere.

16
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:59.620
The baseline is to make service identity, channel security, certificate validation, and operational lifecycle visible and testable across development, staging, and production environments.
